ing molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 1","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":84},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":84},{"title":"A diagram of two cells with the caption a natural killer, NK, immune cell recognizes a healthy cell of the body by binding to a “self” marker on the cell’s surface. The cell on the left is labeled NK cell and it has a small structure with a circular end extending from the cell membrane. The circular end of the small structure is joined to a receptor on the cell membrane of the cell on the right. The cell on the right is labeled healthy cell.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/8dd01535f30c44b2369198ff727bf85a1d569a61.png","line":88,"embedded":true},{"title":"Adaptive immune response: Figure 7","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/GFy_h8cu@9.87:etZobsU-@6/Adaptive-Immune-Response","line":90},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":90}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
