"smart_blocks:Wiki of My Life/Persiapan Belajar Kelas XI/Biologi/Cells Cycles and Communications.md#In unicellular": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"yuogi5","at":1785671881174},"key":"Wiki of My Life/Persiapan Belajar Kelas XI/Biologi/Cells Cycles and Communications.md#In unicellular","lines":[92,214],"size":11318,"outlinks":[{"title":"A photograph of a Hawaiian bobtail squid.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/6058115759ee35aad7691cd4c7d11a5f34ab6499.png","line":20,"embedded":true},{"title":"CC BY 4.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","line":22},{"title":"Euprymna scolopes","target":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euprymna_scolopes_-_image.pbio.v12.i02.g001.png","line":22},{"title":"Diagram of quorum sensing.\nFirst panel: low cell density. When cell density is low, autoinducers diffuse away from the cell.\nSecond panel: high cell density. When cell density is high, more autoinducers are present. They bind to receptors that regulate the transcription of certain genes. Genes responsible for the production of autoinducers are expressed, resulting in a positive-feedback loop.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/0d72bb91bed6766ce0d763203d6be2e5c7f3371b.png","line":45,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling in single-celled organisms: Figure 2","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.85:49/Signaling-in-Single-Celled-Org","line":49},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/us/","line":49},{"title":"Image of a _Staphylococcus aureus_ biofilm on the surface of a catheter.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/6cfccacb8d1b2d777f412f7b213935a11b45df3e.png","line":57,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling in single-celled organisms: Figure 3","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.85:49/Signaling-in-Single-Celled-Org","line":59},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":59},{"title":"Micrograph of yeast cells.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/1262007f1e03fb606f25658b382523d7027c6b57.png","line":67,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling in single-celled organisms: Figure 1","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.85:49/Signaling-in-Single-Celled-Org","line":69},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":69},{"title":"cell signaling in yeast reproduction","target":"https://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/cell-signaling/signaling-between-single-celled-organisms/v/cell-signaling-in-yeast-reproduction","line":73},{"title":"A photomicrograph of a budding yeast cell.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/906e6cb13c6faecc0088ae87cf6feba838344abd.png","line":75,"embedded":true},{"title":"Shmoo yeast, S. cerevisiae","target":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shmoo_yeast_S_cerevisiae.jpg","line":77},{"title":"G protein-coupled receptor","target":"https://www.khanacademy.org/science/ap-biology/cell-communication-and-cell-cycle/signal-transduction/v/g-protein-coupled-receptors","line":79},{"title":"growth factor signaling","target":"https://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/cell-signaling/mechanisms-of-cell-signaling/v/example-of-signal-transduction-pathway","line":79}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
