 through cell-cell contact":[76,91],"#In normal circumstances#Autocrine signaling#Signaling through cell-cell contact#{1}":[78,91],"#In unicellular":[92,214],"#In unicellular#Introduction":[93,100],"#In unicellular#Introduction#{1}":[95,100],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria":[101,153],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#{1}":[103,106],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Quorum sensing in symbiosis":[107,116],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Quorum sensing in symbiosis#{1}":[109,116],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Mechanisms of quorum sensing":[117,143],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Mechanisms of quorum sensing#{1}":[119,124],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Mechanisms of quorum sensing#{2}":[125,126],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Mechanisms of quorum sensing#{3}":[127,128],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Mechanisms of quorum sensing#{4}":[129,130],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Mechanisms of quorum sensing#{5}":[131,133],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Mechanisms of quorum sensing#{6}":[134,143],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Quorum sensing and biofilms":[144,153],"#In unicellular#Quorum sensing in bacteria#Quorum sensing and biofilms#{1}":[146,153],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts":[154,214],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{1}":[156,175],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{2}":[176,177],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{3}":[178,179],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{4}":[180,181],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{5}":[182,183],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{6}":[184,185],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{7}":[186,188],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{8}":[189,190],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{9}":[191,193],"#In unicellular#Signaling in yeasts#{10}":[194,214]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Sending cell: this cell secretes a ligand.\nTarget cell: this cell has a receptor that can bind the ligand. The ligand binds to the receptor and triggers a signaling cascade inside the cell, leading to a response.\nNontarget cell: this cell does not have a receptor for the ligand (though it may have other kinds of receptors). The cell does not perceive the ligand and thus does not respond to it.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/78e5a101b76d273bf4051ceaba32dfc042e0f891.png","line":12,"embedded":true},{"title":"A sketch of the cell with a caption that says chemical messengers relay the signal. Outside the cell there is a small circle labeled ligand and an arrow from the ligand is pointing toward a structure on the cell membrane labeled receptor. Inside the cell there is an arrow pointing from the receptor towards an unlabeled structure and from the unlabeled structure there is another arrow pointing towards a second unlabeled structure. From the second unlabeled structure inside the cell there is an arrow pointing toward the word response.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/72d570876a67f6c4012f5900a66878d4e21b5407.png","line":20,"embedded":true},{"title":"cellular responses","target":"https://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/cell-signaling/mechanisms-of-cell-signaling/a/cellular-response","line":22},{"title":"signal relay","target":"https://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/cell-signaling/mechanisms-of-cell-signaling/a/intracellular-signal-transduction","line":22},{"title":"ligands and receptors","target":"https://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/cell-signaling/mechanisms-of-cell-signaling/a/signal-perception","line":22},{"title":"Cross-section of the developing spinal cord, showing the distribution of Shh and the specification of different types of neurons. As Shh diffuses away from the notochord and floor plate, it forms a gradient, with high levels near the source and low levels further away. The different concentrations of Shh at different points along the gradient help tell nearby cells what types of neurons they should become.\n* Cells that are close to the notochord and floor plate receive a high dose of signal and become a specific type of connector neuron (interneuron).\n* Cells that are a little further away get a lower dose of signal and become motor neurons (neurons that connect up to muscles).\n* Cell that are even more distant from the notochord  and floor plate receive progressively lower doses of signal and become other types of interneurons.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/015acb37fcbc089ce3ff54b98d5bd729b0cdf4e0.png","line":36,"embedded":true},{"title":"Synaptic signaling. Neurotransmitter is released from vesicles at the end of the axon of the sending cell. It diffuses across the small gap between sending and target neurons and binds to receptors on the target neuron.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/a034cb7d0abe5fce1a8682a8ba5dd32fe5e1617c.png","line":47,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 2","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":49},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":49},{"title":"Paracrine signaling: a cell targets a nearby cell (one not attached by gap junctions). The image shows a signaling molecule produced by one cell diffusing a short distance to a neighboring cell.\nAutocrine signaling: a cell targets itself, releasing a signal that can bind to receptors on its own surface.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/df4dfd3468dca5f91c819a91ecd2721834987bc4.png","line":53,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 1","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":56},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":56},{"title":"Endocrine signaling: a cell targets a distant cell through the bloodstream. A signaling molecule is released by one cell, then travels through the bloodstream to bind to receptors on a distant target cell elsewhere in the body.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/6edce2c1524f619c2536ca439d3c26fecc3a3847.png","line":72,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 2","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":74},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":74},{"title":"Signaling across gap junctions. A cell targets a neighboring cell connected via gap junctions. Signals travel from one cell to the other by passing through the gap junctions.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/dd8b8b06a33673ebcf2532628e8e24d060f52647.png","line":82,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 1","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":84},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":84},{"title":"A diagram of two cells with the caption a natural killer, NK, immune cell recognizes a healthy cell of the body by binding to a “self” marker on the cell’s surface. The cell on the left is labeled NK cell and it has a small structure with a circular end extending from the cell membrane. The circular end of the small structure is joined to a receptor on the cell membrane of the cell on the right. The cell on the right is labeled healthy cell.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/8dd01535f30c44b2369198ff727bf85a1d569a61.png","line":88,"embedded":true},{"title":"Adaptive immune response: Figure 7","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/GFy_h8cu@9.87:etZobsU-@6/Adaptive-Immune-Response","line":90},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":90},{"title":"A photograph of a Hawaiian bobtail squid.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/6058115759ee35aad7691cd4c7d11a5f34ab6499.png","line":111,"embedded":true},{"title":"CC BY 4.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","line":113},{"title":"Euprymna scolopes","target":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euprymna_scolopes_-_image.pbio.v12.i02.g001.png","line":113},{"title":"Diagram of quorum sensing.\nFirst panel: low cell density. When cell density is low, autoinducers diffuse away from the cell.\nSecond panel: high cell density. When cell density is high, more autoinducers are present. They bind to receptors that regulate the transcription of certain genes. Genes responsible for the production of autoinducers are expressed, resulting in a positive-feedback loop.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/0d72bb91bed6766ce0d763203d6be2e5c7f3371b.png","line":136,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling in single-celled organisms: Figure 2","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.85:49/Signaling-in-Single-Celled-Org","line":140},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/us/","line":140},{"title":"Image of a _Staphylococcus aureus_ biofilm on the surface of a catheter.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/6cfccacb8d1b2d777f412f7b213935a11b45df3e.png","line":148,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling in single-celled organisms: Figure 3","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.85:49/Signaling-in-Single-Celled-Org","line":150},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":150},{"title":"Micrograph of yeast cells.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/1262007f1e03fb606f25658b382523d7027c6b57.png","line":158,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling in single-celled organisms: Figure 1","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.85:49/Signaling-in-Single-Celled-Org","line":160},