f the axon of the sending cell. It diffuses across the small gap between sending and target neurons and binds to receptors on the target neuron.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/a034cb7d0abe5fce1a8682a8ba5dd32fe5e1617c.png","line":18,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 2","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":20},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":20},{"title":"Paracrine signaling: a cell targets a nearby cell (one not attached by gap junctions). The image shows a signaling molecule produced by one cell diffusing a short distance to a neighboring cell.\nAutocrine signaling: a cell targets itself, releasing a signal that can bind to receptors on its own surface.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/df4dfd3468dca5f91c819a91ecd2721834987bc4.png","line":24,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 1","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":27},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":27}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
