45217,0.00986117,-0.04415961,0.05024331,0.03594046,-0.00043144,0.05607837,0.08120089,-0.06569126,-0.03834064,-0.04198013,0.04164228,-0.01318416,0.18378,-0.05921585,0.08465511,0.06440438,-0.03343389,0.02004636,0.05749926,0.00542078,0.02594098,0.07148185,-0.04726427,-0.03823575,0.01196352,0.05029354,0.00951766,0.03112977,-0.03122837,0.00899902,0.01547032,0.01036945,-0.02740851,-0.02655533,0.12263505,-0.03649718,-0.04716385,-0.03037184,0.0480044,0.01142029,-0.04734738,0.00663348,-0.08601779,-0.02143191,-0.03416304,-0.00723998,-0.00730709,-0.01287004,-0.02561756,0.06045571,0.02275047,-0.03160881,-0.00792736,0.09304424,0.01135855],"last_embed":{"tokens":393,"hash":"9jym4w"}}},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"9jym4w","at":1785671935582},"key":"Wiki of My Life/Persiapan Belajar Kelas XI/Biologi/Cells Cycles and Communications.md#In normal circumstances#Autocrine signaling","lines":[58,91],"size":5078,"outlinks":[{"title":"Endocrine signaling: a cell targets a distant cell through the bloodstream. A signaling molecule is released by one cell, then travels through the bloodstream to bind to receptors on a distant target cell elsewhere in the body.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/6edce2c1524f619c2536ca439d3c26fecc3a3847.png","line":15,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 2","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":17},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":17},{"title":"Signaling across gap junctions. A cell targets a neighboring cell connected via gap junctions. Signals travel from one cell to the other by passing through the gap junctions.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/dd8b8b06a33673ebcf2532628e8e24d060f52647.png","line":25,"embedded":true},{"title":"Signaling molecules and cellular receptors: Figure 1","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/185cbf87-c72e-48f5-b51e-f14f21b5eabd@9.87:46/Signaling-Molecules-and-Cellul","line":27},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":27},{"title":"A diagram of two cells with the caption a natural killer, NK, immune cell recognizes a healthy cell of the body by binding to a “self” marker on the cell’s surface. The cell on the left is labeled NK cell and it has a small structure with a circular end extending from the cell membrane. The circular end of the small structure is joined to a receptor on the cell membrane of the cell on the right. The cell on the right is labeled healthy cell.","target":"https://cdn.kastatic.org/ka-perseus-images/8dd01535f30c44b2369198ff727bf85a1d569a61.png","line":31,"embedded":true},{"title":"Adaptive immune response: Figure 7","target":"http://cnx.org/contents/GFy_h8cu@9.87:etZobsU-@6/Adaptive-Immune-Response","line":33},{"title":"CC BY 3.0","target":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","line":33}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
